## 3.2.0 (unreleased)

Breaking changes:
* The configuration-file extensions no longer use Ocsigen Server's
  `<datadir>`, which is going away. `ocsipersist-dbm-config` now requires
  `<store dir="..."/>`, and `ocsipersist-sqlite-config` requires
  `<database file="..."/>`. Without the tag, the server refuses to start
  with a clear error, instead of storing the data in a directory chosen when
  Ocsigen Server was built.
* `ocsipersist-dbm-config` follows Ocsigen Server 8.1, where there is no log
  directory unless the configuration gives one. Without it, ocsidbm writes
  its errors to the standard error of the server. This needs Ocsigen Server
  8.1, whose `Messages.error_log_path` returns an option.
* In library mode (without a configuration file), there are no default
  paths anymore. The SQLite database file must be set with
  `Ocsipersist_settings.set_db_file`, and the DBM store directory with
  `Ocsipersist_settings.set_store`. Otherwise `Ocsipersist.init` (or, with
  SQLite, the first access to the database) fails with an error naming the
  setter, instead of using `ocsidb` or `ocsipersist-store` in the current
  directory.
  Without `Ocsipersist_settings.set_error_log_path`, ocsidbm writes its
  errors to the standard error of the program, instead of
  `ocsipersist-errors`.
* `Ref.ref`, `Ref_json.ref` and `Store_json.make_persistent` no longer
  access the database. The value is looked up, and its default written if
  it is missing, at the first `get` or `set`. A database error is reported
  there too, and no longer when the value is created.

Bug fixes:
* Persistent values created before the backend is configured, for instance
  when a module is initialised before the program calls
  `Ocsipersist_settings.set_db_file`, or `Ocsipersist.init` with DBM, now
  work, since they access the database at their first use. A failed
  access, for instance before the configuration, is tried again by the
  next one. Before, it broke the value for good, and with `Ref` every
  persistent reference of the program. The first access to a table
  (`Functorial.Table`, `Polymorphic.open_table`) is also tried again after
  a failure with SQLite and PostgreSQL.

## 3.1.2 (2026-09-27)

Packaging:
* Require OCaml >= 4.14 in ocsipersist-lib (it uses `String.for_all`).
* Tests: the DBM tests run on Arch Linux (no `which`) and FreeBSD
  (`waitpid` interrupted by `SIGCHLD`).

## 3.1.1 (2026-09-26)

Packaging:
* Require lwt >= 5.7.0: ocsipersist-lib uses `Lwt.reraise` since 2.1.0.
* The PostgreSQL backend test, which starts a local server, now runs only
  when `OCSIPERSIST_TEST_PGSQL=true` is set, so building
  ocsipersist-pgsql with tests no longer fails where no server can be
  started.

## 3.1.0 (2026-09-25)

New features:
* New exception `Decoding_error`, raised by the JSON frontends (`Ref_json`,
  `Store_json`, `Column.Json`) when a stored value cannot be deserialised by
  the codec it was opened with. Backend errors keep their own exceptions, so
  callers can now distinguish an unreadable value from a database failure.

Bug fixes:
* `Store_json.make_persistent` (and its lazy variants) no longer fails when
  the existing stored value cannot be deserialised. The value is left in
  place; `get` reports it with `Decoding_error` and the caller can overwrite
  it with `set`. Previously such a value made `make_persistent` fail with no
  way to recover, since overwriting requires the handle it failed to return.

## 3.0.0 (2026-09-25)

Breaking changes:
* Adapt the Ocsigen Server configuration-file extensions
  (`ocsipersist-*-config`) to the module names introduced in Ocsigen Server 8
  (`Ocsigen.Extensions`, `Ocsigen.Config`, `Ocsigen.Messages`). These packages
  now require `ocsigenserver >= 8.0.0 & < 9.0.0`.

Packaging:
* `ocsipersist-dbm` and `ocsipersist-dbm-config` are now marked as unavailable
  on Windows and macOS: the DBM backend relies on a separate `ocsidbm` process,
  Unix-domain sockets and gdbm/ndbm.

## 2.1.0 (2026-06-13)

New features:
* Type-safe JSON serialisation via `Deriving_Json`: new modules `Store_json`,
  `Ref_json` and functor `Column.Json`, available in all backends as
  alternatives to the `Marshal`-based `Store`, `Ref` and `Column.Marshal`.
  Persisted data is human-readable and stable across OCaml versions. This adds
  a dependency on `js_of_ocaml` (for the `Deriving_Json` runtime only; no
  JavaScript compilation is involved).
* `iter` and `fold` added to the `Polymorphic` interface; `iter_step` and
  `fold_step` are now deprecated.
* `iter_batch` implemented for the SQLite and DBM backends (was raising at
  runtime).

Bug fixes:
* SQLite: keep a persistent database connection instead of opening and closing
  it on every operation.
* PostgreSQL: fix `replace_if_exists` (`raise` instead of `Lwt.fail`), wrap
  `modify_opt` in a transaction, fix `Functorial.length` (was using `Marshal`
  on a `count` result) and fix `Functorial.fold`/`Polymorphic.fold` returning
  the initial accumulator.
* DBM: replace the `Lwt_unix.fork` double-fork with `Unix.create_process`,
  compatible with OCaml 5 multicore domains.

Security:
* Validate table and store names (only `[a-zA-Z0-9_]` allowed) in all backends
  to prevent SQL injection through interpolated names.

Other:
* Add a test suite for the three backends (SQLite, PostgreSQL, DBM) and a CI
  workflow running it.

## 2.0.0 (2024-06-15)

See the git history for changes up to this release.
